@startuml affinity-decision-tree
'=============================================================================
' Turso affinity — unified decision tree
'
' Converted from the "unified decision tree" section of
' affinity-rules-reference.md (its interactive treedata.json).
'
' Two boxes in the real flow are walked more than once:
'
'   $column_affinity  (§2)   — resolve a column's own declared affinity.
'                              Walked 5 times: once on the write path, once
'                              per comparison operand, and once per index
'                              affinity check (text and numeric).
'   $operand_affinity (§3.1) — resolve an expression's ExprAffinityInfo.
'                              Walked twice, once for LHS and once for RHS.
'
' They are written once here as preprocessor procedures and expanded at every
' call site, so every copy on the rendered diagram stays identical.
'
' Render:  java -jar plantuml.jar docs/internals/affinity-decision-tree.puml
'=============================================================================

title Turso affinity — unified decision tree

skinparam defaultTextAlignment left
skinparam ConditionEndStyle hline
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11
skinparam PartitionBorderColor #7F8C8D
skinparam PartitionFontStyle bold
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #FEF9E7
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FDF6E3
skinparam NoteBorderColor #B7950B

legend top left
  |<#E8F6F3>  | a resolved affinity value |
  |<#D6EAF8>  | an ExprAffinityInfo result (has_affinity + affinity) |
  |<#F9E79F>  | an override that beats whatever was just computed |
  |<#F5B7B1>  | a known bug or a deliberate deviation from SQLite |
  |<#ABEBC6>  | terminal outcome |
endlegend


'=============================================================================
' §2 — resolve a column's own declared affinity
'
' $who names the column being resolved, so each of the five expansions is
' labelled with the context it was reached from.
'=============================================================================
!procedure $column_affinity($who)
partition "§2 · Resolve $who own declared affinity" {

  :Start from $who declared type string;
  note right
    This first question only matters for STRICT tables using a
    custom/domain type (registered via CREATE TYPE) or an array
    column (e.g. INTEGER[]).

    Turso needs the type's real base affinity //before// running the
    substring rules, because those rules can be fooled by a custom
    type's name — a domain named "doubled" contains "DOUB", which
    the rules below would wrongly read as REAL. The override
    sidesteps that by carrying the correct affinity directly.

    Not a STRICT table, or a plain built-in type like
    INTEGER / TEXT / REAL? Then the answer is always No.
  end note

  if (Does the column have a Turso custom-type\noverride with a packed affinity? (§2)) then (yes)
    #E8F6F3:Use that override's affinity directly —
    skip the substring cascade entirely;
  else (no)

    '--- §2.1 the substring cascade
    if (§2.1 · Affinity::affinity(declared_type) —\nchecked top to bottom, first match wins.\nDoes the declared type contain "INT"?) then (yes)
      #E8F6F3:**INTEGER**;
      note right
        Substring match, not a keyword match —
        e.g. "POINTER" would also match.
      end note
    else (no)
      if (Contains "CHAR", "CLOB" or "TEXT"?) then (yes)
        #E8F6F3:**TEXT**;
      else (no)
        if (Contains "BLOB",\nor is the declared type string empty\n(no type at all)?) then (yes)
          #E8F6F3:**BLOB**;
        else (no)
          if (Contains "REAL", "FLOA" or "DOUB"?) then (yes)
            #E8F6F3:**REAL**;
          else (no)
            #E8F6F3:**NUMERIC**
            (the default — none of the other
            four substrings matched);
          endif
        endif
      endif
    endif

  endif

  '--- §2.2 the STRICT ANY escape hatch
  if (§2.2 · Is the table STRICT and the\ndeclared type exactly "ANY"?) then (yes)
    #F9E79F:Forced to **BLOB**, overriding
    whatever was just computed;
    note right
      STRICT tables (CREATE TABLE ... STRICT) require every column to
      have one of a fixed list of real types. ANY is the one escape
      hatch: a STRICT column declared ANY can hold a value of any
      type, completely unmodified.

      Since it can hold anything, Turso does not try to parse or
      convert it at all — it is always treated as BLOB, meaning
      "keep the bytes exactly as given".

      CREATE TABLE t(x ANY) STRICT;
      x always behaves as BLOB here, no matter what is put in it.
    end note
  else (no)
    :Keep the affinity computed above;
  endif

}
!endprocedure


'=============================================================================
' §3.1 — resolve one comparison operand's ExprAffinityInfo
'
' $side is "LHS" or "RHS". The RHS walk is the identical tree fed the RHS
' expression, which is why one procedure covers both.
'=============================================================================
!procedure $operand_affinity($side)
partition "§3.1 · Resolve $side's ExprAffinityInfo { has_affinity, affinity }" {

  :Look at the $side expression;
  note right
    has_affinity is really asking: does this expression have a REAL,
    trustworthy declared type?

    A table column does. A literal like 5 or 'abc', a computed
    expression like a + 1, or a function call result does not —
    Turso cannot know its type just by looking at it, so for
    comparison purposes it is treated as untyped
    (has_affinity = false, affinity = BLOB), no matter what value it
    evaluates to at runtime.
  end note

  '--- Transparent wrappers recurse into the inner expression. The JSON
  '--- models this as an edge back into this same node; unwrapping in a
  '--- loop first is the same thing and reads better as a graph.
  while (Is it a Parenthesized([e]) with a single element,\nor a Collate(e, _)?) is (yes)
    :Transparent — recurse into e and inherit its answer verbatim.
    COLLATE's own name plays no role in this decision (§4.6);
  endwhile (no)

  switch (What kind of expression is it?)

  case (Resolved Column)
    if (Is this column backed by a real BTree table?\n(the exact-token "INTEGER PRIMARY KEY"\nrowid-alias check also happens here, §7)) then (yes)
      :has_affinity = true — read the column's own declared
      affinity with the same box the WRITE path uses, just
      being read here instead of applied to a new value;
      note right
        "Backed by a real BTree table" is asking: is this an actual
        column of an actual on-disk table (CREATE TABLE), rather than
        a column that only exists because of how the query is written?

        In SELECT ... FROM t1, t1.a is backed by a real BTree — Yes.
        In SELECT ... FROM (SELECT 1 AS x) d, d.x is not: it only
        exists inside this query, so it is a FromClauseSubquery
        column (same for a CTE or a VIEW) — No.
      end note
      $column_affinity("this $side column's")
    else (no — Virtual, FromClauseSubquery\n(FROM-subquery / CTE / VIEW), or RecursiveCteInput)
      #D6EAF8:§10 · has_affinity = true, affinity = whatever was
      BAKED IN when this derived column was first constructed;
      if (Did it come from a single SELECT arm,\nor a compound SELECT?) then (single arm —\nplain derived table / CTE / VIEW,\nno compound operator)
        #F5B7B1:§10.1 · infer_type_from_expr(inner_expr, resolver=None)
        → lossy get_expr_affinity(inner_expr, ..., None).
        resolver is ALWAYS None here, so if inner_expr is itself a
        scalar subquery it can never match the SubqueryResult arm and
        always falls to BLOB, whatever the real inner type is
        — **bug, doc summary #5**;
      else (compound SELECT —\nUNION / UNION ALL / EXCEPT / INTERSECT,\nalgorithm identical for all four operators)
        #F5B7B1:§10.2 · Start at arm 1's affinity via the same lossy
        function. WHILE it reads BLOB, skip to the next arm and adopt
        ITS affinity instead — **bug, doc summary #8**: a genuinely
        BLOB-declared leading arm is indistinguishable from
        "no affinity" here and gets skipped too.
        Once an affinity settles, OR in the remaining arms' storage-class
        masks: a Text-vs-numeric conflict downgrades the result to BLOB,
        otherwise keep the settled value;
      endif
    endif

  case (RowId / ROWID(...) /\n_ROWID_ / OID)
    #D6EAF8:has_affinity = true, affinity = **INTEGER**, always —
    this branch never looks at any column at all (§7);

  case (CAST(expr AS T))
    if (Is T given?) then (yes)
      #D6EAF8:has_affinity = true, **Affinity::affinity(T)** —
      expr's own affinity is NEVER consulted (§8);
    else (no — rare AST form)
      #D6EAF8:has_affinity = false, **BLOB**;
    endif

  case (Literal\n(number / string / blob / NULL /\nTRUE|FALSE / date-time keyword))
    #D6EAF8:has_affinity = false, **BLOB**
    (the "no affinity" sentinel value);

  case (Register\n(UPDATE rewrote a column\nreference to a register))
    if (Is this register mapping found in the resolver?) then (yes)
      #D6EAF8:has_affinity = true, the looked-up affinity;
    else (no)
      #D6EAF8:has_affinity = false, **BLOB**;
    endif
    note right
      An internal detail, not something you write in SQL. When
      translating something like UPDATE t SET a = a + 1, Turso
      sometimes needs to compare against the column's in-progress new
      value before it is written back to the table, and that value
      lives in a VDBE register rather than being read fresh from the
      column. You only hit this branch reading generated bytecode.
    end note

  case (Scalar SubqueryResult\n(single-column subquery result))
    if (Found in the resolver, and exactly 1 result register\n(not a multi-column row-value)?) then (yes)
      #D6EAF8:Inherit the inner query's result column's answer
      VERBATIM, has_affinity bit included — this is the one place
      a subquery's real type survives intact;
    else (no — not found, or a\nmulti-column row-value result)
      #D6EAF8:has_affinity = false, **BLOB**;
    endif

  case (Anything else)
    #D6EAF8:has_affinity = false, **BLOB**;
    note right
      No dedicated handling exists for ANY of these forms — they all
      fall to this one wildcard arm:

      Binary / arithmetic / concat / bitwise, FunctionCall(Star),
      Case, Between, InList / InSelect / InTable, IsNull, NotNull,
      Like, Unary, Variable, unresolved Column / Id, Exists,
      DoublyQualified, Name, Qualified, Raise, bare Subquery,
      multi-element Parenthesized, Array, Subscript, Default.
    end note

  endswitch

}
!endprocedure


'=============================================================================
' The tree itself
'=============================================================================
start

if (What are you tracing?) then (writing a value into a column\n(INSERT / UPDATE / UPSERT))

  '=========================================================================
  ' WRITE PATH
  '=========================================================================
  $column_affinity("the target column's")

  partition "§5.3 · apply_affinity_char(value, this affinity)" {
    if (Is the value already Blob?) then (yes)
      :No-op — stored exactly as given;
    else (no)
      if (Is the target affinity Blob?) then (yes)
        :No-op — stored exactly as given;
      else (no)
        if (Is the target affinity Text?) then (yes)
          :A numeric value gets stringified;
        else (no)
          if (Is the target affinity Integer or Numeric?) then (yes)
            :Text gets trimmed and hex ("0x...") is excluded, then parsed
            ONLY if the ENTIRE remaining string is a well-formed number
            (try_for_float, §5.4) — otherwise left as text unchanged;
          else (no — target is Real)
            :An integer is unconditionally converted to float. Text follows
            the same trim/parse rule, but a successful parse always becomes
            a float — it never stays an int, unlike the Integer/Numeric case;
          endif
        endif
      endif
    endif
  }

  #ABEBC6:**DONE — value written**
  The new value is physically written with this coercion applied;
  note right
    STRICT tables skip this entire box and run Insn::TypeCheck
    instead — reject a type mismatch outright rather than coerce it.
  end note
  stop

else (comparing two expressions\n(=, <>, <, <=, >, >=, IS, IN, JOIN ON,\nor preparing an index-seek key))

  '=========================================================================
  ' COMPARE PATH
  '=========================================================================
  $operand_affinity("LHS")

  :Now resolve the RHS the exact same way — walk the identical tree,
  starting again from "is it a resolved Column?", but feeding it the
  RHS expression instead;

  $operand_affinity("RHS")

  partition "§4.1 · COMBINE — comparison_affinity_from_info(LHS, RHS)" {
    if (Do BOTH LHS and RHS have has_affinity = true?) then (yes)
      if (Is either side's affinity numeric\n(Integer / Real / Numeric)?) then (yes)
        #E8F6F3:**NUMERIC**;
      else (no — both Text, both Blob, or one of each)
        #F5B7B1:**BLOB**
        Deviation from SQLite: the textbook rule picks TEXT here if
        either side is TEXT. This always picks BLOB instead, even for
        TEXT-vs-TEXT — **doc summary #1**;
      endif
    else (no)
      switch (Does exactly one side have has_affinity = true?)
      case (only LHS has it)
        if (Is LHS's affinity numeric?) then (yes)
          #E8F6F3:**NUMERIC** — the #6715 downgrade, which stops a lone
          REAL/INTEGER column affinity from surviving onto a no-affinity
          literal and forcing it to f64 before it is even compared;
        else (no)
          #E8F6F3:**LHS.affinity**, unchanged;
        endif
      case (only RHS has it)
        if (Is RHS's affinity numeric?) then (yes)
          #E8F6F3:**NUMERIC** — the #6715 downgrade, mirrored for RHS;
        else (no)
          #E8F6F3:**RHS.affinity**, unchanged;
        endif
      case (neither has it)
        #E8F6F3:**BLOB** — nothing on either side to coerce toward;
      endswitch
    endif
  }

  :ONE resolved Affinity value falls out here.
  If this comparison came from a WHERE/ON term it is cached on the
  Constraint right now (§9.2) — every consumer below reads this same
  cached value, none of them recompute it;

  if (What consumes it?) then (a PLAIN comparison actually\nbeing executed, not just planned)

    '-----------------------------------------------------------------
    ' PLAIN COMPARISON
    '-----------------------------------------------------------------
    partition "§4.3–4.5 · Executing a plain comparison" {
      if (§4.4 · Is either operand NULL?) then (yes)
        #ABEBC6:**NULL short-circuit**
        Short-circuit on the null_eq / jump_if_null flags right now —
        the affinity value computed above is NEVER consulted for this
        pair of operands (§4.5);
        stop
      else (no)
        if (§4.4 · Are both operands already plain integers?) then (yes)
          #ABEBC6:**Fast path**
          Direct compare, no affinity work at all;
          stop
        else (no)
          :§4.3 · Affinity::convert_for_compare(...) runs
          INDEPENDENTLY on both operands;
          if (Is an operand Text?) then (yes)
            #ABEBC6:**Text operand coerced**
            Parsed/stringified using the exact same rules as the
            apply_affinity_char box at the top of this tree
            (Text / Integer / Numeric / Real target cases).
            Any operand that WAS converted has its register physically
            overwritten with the coerced value as a side effect;
            stop
          else (no)
            if (Is the operand already numeric\nand the target affinity Real?) then (yes)
              #ABEBC6:**Left alone**
              No forced int→float. This is what the #6695 fix changed
              relative to storage's Affinity::convert, and it is
              why #6056 / #6272 do not reproduce here anymore;
              stop
            else (no — target is Blob)
              #ABEBC6:**No-op**
              Target is Blob → no-op regardless of the operand;
              stop
            endif
          endif
        endif
      endif
    }

  else (an INDEX SEEK — deciding whether an index can\ndrive this predicate, then coercing the seek key if so)

    '-----------------------------------------------------------------
    ' INDEX SEEK
    '-----------------------------------------------------------------
    switch (§9.1 · index_affinity_ok —\nwhat is the resolved comparison affinity?)

    case (BLOB)
      :index_affinity_ok = true, unconditionally — any index column
      affinity is safe against a BLOB comparison;

    case (TEXT)
      :Only ok if the INDEXED COLUMN's own affinity is ALSO Text —
      walk the "resolve the column's own declared affinity" box
      again, this time for the indexed column;
      $column_affinity("the indexed column's")
      if (Is the indexed column's own affinity Text?) then (yes)
        :index_affinity_ok = true;
      else (no)
        #F5B7B1:**INDEX PRUNED**
        This index is excluded from cost-based selection entirely for
        this predicate, before cost-based selection even runs. Exactly
        the same check, reading the exact same cached Constraint value,
        whether the index came from a real CREATE INDEX or an
        optimizer-synthesized auto-index for an unindexed join side (§9.3);
        stop
      endif

    case (Numeric\n(Integer / Real / Numeric))
      :Only ok if the indexed column's own affinity is ALSO numeric —
      otherwise the column's stored values were coerced to text at
      insert time and a numeric seek key could never match them
      by memcmp;
      $column_affinity("the indexed column's")
      if (Is the indexed column's own affinity numeric\n(Integer / Real / Numeric)?) then (yes)
        :index_affinity_ok = true;
      else (no)
        #F5B7B1:**INDEX PRUNED**
        The column's stored values were coerced to text at insert time,
        so a numeric seek key could never match them by memcmp. The
        index is excluded from cost-based selection entirely for this
        predicate, before cost-based selection even runs — same check
        for a real CREATE INDEX and for an optimizer-synthesized
        auto-index on an unindexed join side (§9.3);
        stop
      endif

    endswitch

    #ABEBC6:**SEEK PROCEEDS** (§9.4)
    Insn::Affinity is emitted on the seek-key register(s), coercing the
    key toward the resolved comparison affinity using the same
    apply_affinity_char rules from the top of this tree (skipped if
    provably a no-op) — THEN the SeekGE / SeekGT / SeekLE / SeekLT
    opcode actually runs;
    note right
      A range predicate's lower and upper bound are each their own
      independent walk through this entire tree. There is no combined
      "range affinity".
    end note
    stop

  endif

endif

@enduml
